      "featured": true,
      "body": "Every consulting instinct says the same thing: WhatsApp is not a business system, so move the business off WhatsApp.\n\nThe instinct is correct and the advice does not work.\n\n## Why it does not work\n\nBusiness communication in India did not choose WhatsApp for its features. It chose it because everyone was already there — the vendor, the doctor, the site supervisor, the customer who will not download an app for a once-a-year transaction.\n\nAsking a business to move is asking it to also move everyone it deals with. That is not a change management problem, it is a network problem, and a single company cannot solve it alone.\n\n## The alternative\n\nStop trying to move people and put the intelligence where they already are.\n\nThe interface is a message. The system does the structuring. A supervisor sends a photo of a delivery note and the line items reach the sheet. A doctor asks how many patients missed follow-ups this week and gets a list. A hotel manager forwards a guest complaint and it is logged against the booking and escalated.\n\nNobody logs in. Nobody is trained. Adoption is not a phase of the project.\n\n## What works well on this channel\n\nAnything shaped like a request and a response. Reports on demand, reminders and chases, document intake, approvals, escalations, structured data capture through short conversational flows.\n\n## What does not\n\nAnything needing comparison across many items at once. Reviewing thirty candidates, reconciling a large dataset, or exploring a dashboard are all genuinely worse in a chat window, and pretending otherwise produces a bad experience.\n\nThe honest architecture is usually both: WhatsApp for capture, chasing and quick answers; a proper interface for analysis and configuration. Most people spend nearly all their time in the first and visit the second occasionally.\n\n## The unglamorous part\n\nBuilding well on WhatsApp means the Business API, template message approvals, session windows, opt-in compliance and rate limits. It is genuinely more constrained than building a web app, and those constraints exist mostly to protect users from spam — which is why the channel still works.\n\nWorth knowing before assuming this is the cheap option. It usually is not. It is the one people use.",
