      "title": "What AI clinical documentation actually has to get right in India",
      "excerpt": "The model is the easy part. Language switching, corridor noise and the review step are where these systems are won or lost.",
      "seoTitle": "AI Clinical Documentation in India | GooseLabs",
      "seoDescription": "Building AI medical scribes for Indian clinics - handling mixed Hindi-English consultations, clinical safety, review workflow and data residency.",
      "date": "2026-04-21",
      "author": "Palash Kanwar",
      "readingTime": 7,
      "tags": [
        "Healthcare",
        "Clinical AI",
        "EMR"
      ],
      "featured": false,
      "body": "Ambient clinical documentation is one of the few AI applications in healthcare with an obvious case. Doctors spend a great deal of time writing notes. The notes are essential. Writing them is not what anyone trained for.\n\nThe demos are impressive. Deploying one in an Indian clinic is a different exercise, and the gap is not where people expect.\n\n## Consultations are not in one language\n\nA consultation in Raipur moves between Hindi and English within a sentence, and sometimes into Chhattisgarhi. Symptoms are described in local terms. Drug names are pronounced in ways no training set anticipated.\n\nSpeech models trained largely on clean English clinical audio degrade sharply here, and they degrade quietly — producing confident, fluent, wrong text rather than obviously failing.\n\n## The room is loud\n\nClinical audio in the demo is a quiet consulting room with a good microphone. Actual audio has corridor noise, a fan, a phone, and a family member of the patient contributing.\n\nMost of our engineering effort went here rather than into the model.\n\n## A transcript is not a note\n\nHanding a doctor a verbatim transcript makes the problem longer. A clinical note is a structured artefact — history, examination, assessment, plan — and the value is entirely in that structure.\n\nGetting the structure right requires the system to understand which part of a rambling conversation is history and which is assessment. That is harder than transcription and it is the part that determines whether the output is usable.\n\n## The review step is the product\n\nThe design constraint that matters more than accuracy: the system cannot add time to the consultation.\n\nIf a doctor spends three minutes correcting a note, they stop using it inside a week and go back to shorthand. So the interaction has to be review-and-confirm, not read-and-rewrite.\n\nThis also means uncertainty must be surfaced rather than smoothed over. A note flagging three uncertain items is far safer than a fluent note with three silent errors — and clinicians calibrate their trust accordingly, which is exactly what you want.\n\n## Clinical safety is not negotiable\n\nThe clinician is the author. Nothing enters the record without human confirmation. Automated clinical documentation without a review step is a patient safety issue, and no efficiency argument outweighs that.\n\n## Data residency\n\nPatient data should stay on infrastructure the clinic controls, in India. This constrains architecture — including which models can be used and how — and it is far cheaper to design for at the start than to retrofit after a compliance review.\n\n## Where this goes\n\nOnce notes are structured rather than shorthand, the second-order benefits arrive: cohort analysis, protocol comparison, real answers to whether a treatment approach worked.\n\nThat is the actual prize. Saving the doctor an hour is what gets the system adopted.",
